[{:rule-id "rule-ai", :item {:feed-id "hn", :item-id "https://www.greptile.com/blog/trex-computer-use", :title "How should an LLM see a computer?", :link "https://www.greptile.com/blog/trex-computer-use", :published-at #inst "2026-11-23T17:19:25.000-00:00", :content "Comments"}, :excerpts [{:text "How should an LLM see a computer?", :matched-terms ["llm"], :source :title}]} {:rule-id "clojure", :item {:feed-id "planet-clojure", :item-id "https://yogthos.net/posts/2026-11-23-b+trees.html", :title "Swapping Jolt's sorted collections to a B+tree", :link "https://yogthos.net/posts/2026-11-23-b+trees.html", :published-at #inst "2026-11-23T00:00:00.000-00:00", :content "Until recently, sorted-map and sorted-set in Jolt were a straight port of Clojure's PersistentTreeMap, which is backed by a persistent red-black tree. Using a red-black tree has worked well for Clojure, and it provides a solid foundation for backing these data structures. However, red-black trees have a downside, which is that every entry gets its own small node, meaning that every step of a lookup follows a pointer to a different place in memory. So, naturally I got curious to see if a different data structure could be used for the same job which might have a lower overhead. Turns out that a red-black tree can indeed be swapped out for a B+tree here. I updated Jolt's sorted collections along with the java.util.TreeMap and TreeSet shims to use a persistent B+tree, which dropped peak memory by 40% while making most operations faster at every size, as we'll see later in the benchmarks. What was wrong with the red-black tree Each red-black node in Jolt was a five-slot Scheme vector holding a color along with the key, the value, and its two children. And all that translates into 48 bytes of metadata per entry on top of the key and value themselves. A map with 4 million entries needs a tree around 22 levels deep, so, a lookup can end up visiting about 22 nodes scattered across the heap. And once the tree outgrows the CPU cache, each of these visits is likely to be a cache miss as well. As you might've guessed, writes also have a similar problem. Since a persistent tree can't modify a node in place, an assoc has to copy every node on the path from the root to the change, which results in 22 small allocations followed by rebalancing at that size. The B+tree The key advantage of using a B+tree is that it keeps data in leaves of up to 16 entries and branches through inner nodes of up to 32 children with each node being a single Scheme vector. In this layout, a leaf is written as #(#t k0 v0 k1 v1 ...) with keys and values sitting side by side in one contiguous block. An inner node also stores the largest key below it right next to each child. Now, the same tree of 4 million entries becomes just 5 levels deep instead of 22. On top of that, a lookup within each node has to only search through adjacent slots that are already in cache. Writes still have to copy the path from the root, but that's now a quarter of the work with each one being copied as a block. Unsurprisingly, memory use improved significantly, since a full leaf holds 16 entries in 34 machine words, needing only 17 bytes per entry against the red-black tree's 48. Nodes are also allowed to drop to half before they merge, so 34 bytes per entry ends up being the worst case here. It would be ideal to keep the common case near full as much as possible, and a trick that can be used here is to start a fresh node when append goes past either end of the tree rather than splitting a full node into two half-empty ones. A common case for this would be building from ascending keys, which ends up packing every node using this approach. Similarly, deletes end up borrowing entries from a neighbour or merging with it to keep the tree balanced without needing rotations. The catch Of course, nothing in life is free, and B+trees do have a weakness of their own. While they're much faster for large collections, building small ones of tens to hundreds of entries one insert at a time ended up being around 15% slower than the red-black tree. The reason goes back to the respective structure of each data structure. Once a map has 17 entries, that requires a root with two leaves, and every insert needs to copy the whole path. So, at smaller sizes the B+tree has to move more bytes per insert than the red-black tree, which is the trade-off. The numbers Luckily, there is a way around the problem by changing how collections get built. We can first collect the inputs for into, the sorted-map and sorted-set constructors, and multi-argument conj/assoc to check whether they're already in order, then skip the sort if they are. Additionally, we can drop duplicate keys by keeping the first key along with the last value, which allows us to bulk-load the leaves. The batches can now get merged with the collection that already has entries. Meanwhile, small sources of 8 or fewer entries that go into an empty collection continue to use plain conj, because the overhead of buffering would end up costing more than it actually saves. With these changes in place, the worst remaining case ends up being a 4 entry map at 1.05x, which is effectively as good as a red-black tree. So, now that we've discussed how B+trees work, and what makes them a good alternative to red-black trees in this scenario, let's look at some actual benchmark numbers comparing the two approaches. The numbers were measured using optimized binaries across several runs on an M1 Max with 64 GB. The JVM comparisons use Clojure 1.12.6 on OpenJDK 20. A ratio under 1.0 means the B+tree is faster. Small collections Times are for the whole operation over the collection, which includes building it, doing a lookup for every key, removing every key, and replacing every value. Keys are also scrambled to avoid a degenerate sequential case. entries build get all dissoc all assoc (replace) 4 748 → 785 ns (1.05) 342 → 257 ns (0.75) 752 → 740 ns (0.98) 515 → 497 ns (0.97) 16 2.98 → 3.05 µs (1.02) 1.44 → 0.96 µs (0.67) 3.16 → 2.87 µs (0.91) 2.27 → 2.10 µs (0.92) 32 5.99 → 6.25 µs (1.04) 3.03 → 2.22 µs (0.73) 6.80 → 6.47 µs (0.95) 5.06 → 5.10 µs (1.01) 64 13.2 → 13.4 µs (1.01) 6.46 → 4.70 µs (0.73) 14.9 → 13.7 µs (0.92) 11.4 → 10.6 µs (0.93) 256 62.1 → 60.3 µs (0.97) 29.7 → 23.0 µs (0.77) 72.6 → 62.2 µs (0.86) 59.2 → 48.7 µs (0.82) As we can see, lookups win at every size, by 23 to 33%. Builds and writes stay flat below 64 entries. Notably, even a 4 entry map ends up being a single leaf searched in place, meaning that it doesn't pay for the extra machinery. 20,000 entries, operation by operation operation main B+tree ratio get 3.95 ms 3.41 ms 0.86 contains? 3.79 ms 3.20 ms 0.84 get with a custom comparator 10.62 ms 6.59 ms 0.62 dissoc all 12.28 ms 8.22 ms 0.67 disj half 6.55 ms 4.46 ms 0.68 pop-min loop 16.38 ms 9.98 ms 0.61 pop-max loop 24.14 ms 11.33 ms 0.47 seq walk 1.15 ms 1.02 ms 0.89 reduce 0.33 ms 0.23 ms 0.70 reduce-kv 3.02 ms 0.17 ms 0.06 rseq 0.34 ms 0.23 ms 0.68 subseq 0.89 ms 0.77 ms 0.87 subseq, first element only 17.34 ms 7.14 ms 0.41 The reduce-kv result stands out because it's a result of a fix in the new layout. The original red-black tree code built a map entry for every pair going through the generic sequence path. Walking the leaves and passing each key/value pair straight to the function avoids having to allocate memory for each slot. And since the B+tree can seek to a key to start reading from that leaf without having to rebuild a stack of nodes from the root on every call, repeatedly taking the first element of a subseq gets faster as well. 200,000 entries operation ratio build 0.53 get all 0.58 dissoc all 0.55 assoc (replace) 0.60 From here on out we hit a cliff where the cache matters more than anything else. 100,000 to 4 million entries, compared with the JVM While performance looked good for thousands of elements, it was also important to check what would happen with collections having millions of entries in order to make sure nothing broke or regressed badly at this scale. The following benchmark builds a sorted map and a sorted set from scrambled keys and then runs a mix of operations over them. operation, 4M entries main B+tree JVM Clojure into (map) 2,441 ms 1,841 ms 1,659 ms into (set) 2,049 ms 1,619 ms 2,489 ms assoc loop 2,375 ms 2,153 ms 1,762 ms get all 777 ms 660 ms 449 ms contains? all 1,004 ms 659 ms 615 ms dissoc half 2,520 ms 1,662 ms 1,098 ms seq walk 171 ms 131 ms 289 ms reduce over set 63 ms 47 ms 113 ms subseq, 1,000 entries 0.1 ms 0.1 ms 0.4 ms And the good news is that every operation at this scale is also faster than the previous version. Not only that, but Jolt is now faster than the JVM at building sets, walking, reducing, and range queries, while the two are roughly even on contains? and the map build. Finally, point lookups and deletes are still about 1.5x slower than the JVM, which uses a red-black tree backed PersistentTreeMap thanks to the magic of the highly optimized JIT that the JVM enjoys. Peak resident memory for the 4M run nearly halved, going from 1.73 GB to 1.04 GB with the B+tree. It's worth noting that the process holds a 4M entry map with its 4M entry set, along with the garbage from building them. So, the numbers here reflect the whole program rather than just the trees themselves. And the JVM run peaked at 4 to 6 GB, but that's mostly an artifact of how the JVM collects garbage with its default settings, so we shouldn't read too much into that. You might have noticed that the scrambled builds slow down sharply somewhere between 1M and 2M entries. The JVM also shows the same jump with its sort going from 16 to 93 ms. In both cases, the cause is that the working set ends up falling out of cache, making lookup more expensive. java.util.TreeMap As I mentioned earlier, Jolt's TreeMap and TreeSet shims are built on the sorted map. These were implemented using a naive approach where they went through it one generic operation at a time. Each put looked up the key and then inserted it, having to walk the tree twice, while navigation methods like floorKey built a subseq and took its first element. Now, all of these operations have been updated to use the tree directly, meaning that a put or remove is one walk, while operations like floorKey are a single seek. The size of a bounded view can now be computed by adding up whole leaves and only scanning the two partial leaves at the edges. Times are for 20,000 operations. method main B+tree JVM put (fresh map) 34.9 ms 7.5 ms 2.0 ms get 14.5 ms 3.7 ms 1.9 ms floorKey 36.4 ms 4.4 ms 1.7 ms firstKey/lastKey 21.5 ms 1.8 ms 0.4 ms remove then put 87.2 ms 13.8 ms 3.7 ms iterate entries 14.8 ms 3.8 ms 5.8 ms TreeSet add 48.7 ms 7.9 ms 1.8 ms headMap size (200 calls) 189.8 ms 2.7 ms 9.3 ms That's 4 to 70 times faster than the original version, with headMap size beating the JVM, which needs to walk the view in order to count it. But the writes are still about 4x the JVM, and can't be closed with this design because the Java mutates its nodes in place, while Jolt's TreeMap is a mutable wrapper around a persistent tree. So, there's no way around each put having to copy about five nodes. What behaves differently Iteration order, which key is kept on a duplicate, which value wins, the argument order passed to custom comparators, and returning the identical collection from a no-op assoc all work as they did before. But there is one thing that's different from the JVM, which stems from the layout of the trees. Building a 1,024 entry map from ascending keys now calls the comparator 1,023 times while the JVM's red-black tree calls it 12,301 times. So, code that counts comparator calls will see different numbers on Jolt. Takeaways What this exercise shows is the importance of the memory layout used by the backing data structure. All the operations do the same logical work as before, but they now have to touch about a fifth as many nodes with the data inside each node being contiguous. Switching to use a B+tree was not a free win, though, since it required a separate batch build path to make the switch efficient for code that builds lots of medium-sized maps, which adds some complexity to the implementation. If you're making a similar kind of optimization, always be sure to measure the middle sizes along with the extremes."}, :excerpts [{:text "... and sorted-set in Jolt were a straight port of Clojure's PersistentTreeMap, which is backed by a...", :matched-terms ["clojure"], :source :content} {:text "... tree. Using a red-black tree has worked well for Clojure, and it provides a solid foundation for backing...", :matched-terms ["clojure"], :source :content} {:text "... updated Jolt's sorted collections along with the java.util.TreeMap and TreeSet shims to use a...", :matched-terms ["java"], :source :content}]} {:rule-id "clojure", :item {:feed-id "planet-clojure", :item-id "https://medium.com/p/3a9ae4c9782f", :title "Code Mode internals, and adapting them for your CLIs and agent skills", :link "https://muthuishere.medium.com/code-mode-internals-and-adapting-them-for-your-clis-and-agent-skills-3a9ae4c9782f?source=rss-32453dd5d721------2", :published-at #inst "2026-11-23T12:46:46.000-00:00", :content "What exactly happens when Code Mode runs? And Can we adapt the same concept for your CLIs and agent skills? The tool list keeps growing If you look at the new Code Mode in Claude Code, you see that by default the tool list will always grow. The default is a handful of tools. You try to execute things, and then you add your skills, and then your MCP servers, and it keeps on growing. So they introduced Code Mode. What exactly happens when Code Mode runs? Let’s see. What I looked at I looked at the API requests that were made, and the tool list sent in each one. I could see them on my machine. In normal mode, in my session, I get 16 entries. In Code Mode it is just three: REPL, Poll and advisor. The advisor is there in both (you know advisor usually a bigger model like fable will be there..) How Code Mode works The REPL tool carries the descriptions of the tools. Your core built-in tools are there as type signatures, as part of the REPL description. But the description does not have the exact signature of every tool. For the rest there is a method called searchTools. It pulls the tool you need, and then the model writes the code for it. All of this happens in a REPL. If you are into Python or Clojure, or you know what a REPL is, you know it keeps running. Here it keeps running inside that Claude Code session, and the state stays there until the session restarts. With a normal tool call, you need to maintain the state separately and keep track of where you are. The REPL keeps the state between calls , so you do not have to. So I took it as inspiration I have many CLIs and many agent skills. My CLIs have 20 to 30 different ways you can act on. So I thought: adapt the same concept. Make CLI not just expose what it does. Make it composable. What if your CLI could do that? Take aos, a CLI that can do a lot on a system. Move a window, move the mouse, add a bubble, switch something off, open a tab, automate something. There are more than 60 operations. Now say this to an agent, in normal mode or in REPL mode. It has to call the CLI every single time. First aos window move. Then a call to change the brightness. Then another window move. Then aos to open a tab. Each one is a process invocation: you start the process, you get the answer, and then you do the next one. So invoking a CLI is a cost. CLI CodeMode So I thought: if we could have the same concept that Code Mode has, it might be better. I built something called CLI CodeMode. It is a library. You register all your verbs with it, with their descriptions, so the agent can read what each one does. It also gives you JSON output. It also adds one more thing to your CLI, called code. Under code you have describe, and you have skill. It also has a small JavaScript runtime. It is not Bun or Node.js. It runs a JavaScript subset with a small AST. You can import a file and use a regular set of JavaScript and the conditions you need, and nothing more. (I thought of using Clojure, but no one seemed interested, so I changed it to JavaScript. Sorry, TypeScript.) Then you update the skill for it. Think about CLIs that have a lot of commands. We don’t want the agent to keep starting a process for every one of them. Rather, the agent composes one JavaScript program, and the CLI validates it before it runs, with code check, and does the rest. The two CLIs I tried it on ctx-optimize builds a knowledge graph of a codebase(its kind of one of the fastest, despite not many uses), so a coding agent can answer from it instead of grepping files. You ask it to find a symbol, show a card for it, or plan a change. One question usually takes several calls. https://github.com/muthuishere/ctx-optimize aos is CLI over the machine: windows, mouse and keyboard, screenshots, files, shell, packages,bubbles etc. Agents drive the machine with it the same way you do. https://github.com/muthuishere/aos Both are mine, both are released, and both now have a code command. What I measured Take one question to ctx-optimize: search for a symbol, open a card for each of the top five hits, then plan the change. That is seven calls. Here is that work done three ways. Normal: one tool call per command. 3 to 7 trips to the model, 7 processes, 28,273 bytes back. Claude Code’s Code Mode: one script. 1 trip, still 7 processes. The raw output stays in the script. With CLI CodeMode: one code run. 1 trip, 1 process, 169 bytes back, and less CPU (137 ms against 164 to 167 ms). On aos, take a machine check: list the windows, the top processes, the disks, the battery, the running apps and the displays. Six read-only commands, four times each, so 24 calls. Here is that work done three ways: Claude Code Code Mode: 24 processes, 217 to 1,644 ms, 1,228 to 1,493 ms CPU. With CLI CodeMode: 1 process, 217 ms, 1,012 ms CPU. Try it I tried to keep the library small. It is available in Go, Rust, Python and TypeScript. Go is the main one. The others are experimental and run the same engine compiled to WebAssembly. Code Mode removes the trips to the model. It does not remove the process, the parsing and the raw output of every command. That cost sits in the CLI, so the CLI is where to fix it. If your CLI has more than a handful of commands, an agent pays that cost on every call. Add code, describe your operations once, and let the agent write one program. Try it on your own CLI, and if it is not worth it, tell me where it breaks. https://github.com/muthuishere/clicodemode"}, :excerpts [{:text "... I get 16 entries. In Code Mode it is just three: REPL, Poll and advisor. The advisor is there in both...", :matched-terms ["repl"], :source :content} {:text "... fable will be there..) How Code Mode works The REPL tool carries the descriptions of the tools. Your core built-in tools are there as type signatures, as part of the REPL description. But the description does not have...", :matched-terms ["repl"], :source :content} {:text "... writes the code for it. All of this happens in a REPL. If you are into Python or Clojure, or you know what a REPL is, you know it keeps running. Here it keeps...", :matched-terms ["repl" "clojure"], :source :content}]} {:rule-id "rails", :item {:feed-id "alexn-blog", :item-id "https://alexn.org/blog/2026/11/23/humans-md/", :title "HUMANS.md", :link "https://alexn.org/blog/2026/11/23/humans-md/?pk_campaign=rss", :published-at #inst "2026-11-23T14:39:41.000-00:00", :content "Unrelated pic, but I was thinking, wouldn't it be nice if my cats received a HUMANS.md for interacting with us? At work I’m struggling to come up with sane rules for making use of AI. As such, I’ve added the following rules to our repository, meant for people, not AI, maybe you can use it for inspiration as well. NOTE: I am not saying that these rules are the best. Obviously, they are optimized for our team and project. And we’re just getting it started, so it’s a work-in-progress. Keywords like “MUST”, “MUST NOT”, “SHOULD”, “SHOULD NOT” have to be interpreted as described in RFC 2119. Working with AI We follow the No AI Slop manifesto. Outcomes over output; Understanding over speed; Our own thinking over a model’s first draft; Better work over easier work; Improving the system over patching the output. To which we add for clarrification: You MUST own your contributions; we can never blame AI. You MUST respect your colleagues; we must respect their time and their focus, and thus we MUST NOT drown them in slop. Hard rules 1. You MUST write all text meant for humans, not the AI Reasons for this rule: AI prose is most often gibberish; It’s disrespectful of your colleagues, as you couldn’t be bothered to write a couple of words using your own voice, but you’re expecting your colleagues to make an effort in reading that gibberish; If you can’t explain something in your own words, you don’t understand it and thus can’t own your work. Examples of documentation meant for humans: PR descriptions and comments; Code comments; Documentation submitted to ./docs/source; This file. Examples of documentation meant for AI, and that does not necessarily fall under this rule (although you may want manual editing for clarity): ./AGENTS.md ./CONTEXT.md ./CODING_STANDARDS.md ./docs/adr/* ./.agents/** 1.1. You MUST write the PR descriptions and comments As long as we have a human-driven PR review process, PR communication is meant for humans and thus MUST BE written by humans. If you can’t describe what your PR is doing in your own words, you don’t understand your own PR. 1.2. Your code SHOULD have comments, but they MUST NOT be written by AI AGENTS.md already bans AI-driven comments or documentation. Reasons: Comments, when read by AI, become justification for bypassing guardrails and breaking invariants. So the AI can easily convince itself that it did the right thing and then will use that instance to proliferate the decision all over the place. If you can’t be bothered to add comments to the code you’re submitting, for APIs or for problematic pieces, it means you don’t understand your own code. You have to review what your AI is doing anyway; you may as well add your own comments, using your own voice. 2. You MUST do an AI-driven review, locally AI is great at noticing specification-related bugs (e.g., ignored acceptance criteria), or clearly violated coding standards, so this is useful even for PRs that were not executed by AI. Review MUST target these 2 axes: Specifications — access to specs is mandatory, including user stories and other relevant internal documentation; Coding standards (see ./CODING_STANDARDS.md). All PRs MUST have this kind of code review to be merged. But note, don’t make your agent add crappy comments straight on the PR, as it would violate the above rule (PR comments are meant for humans and thus MUST be written by humans). Use code-reviewing, the recommended skill, because it’s optimized for our needs. If you have similar rules in your repo, I’d love to hear about it. The article HUMANS.md first appeared on alexn.org."}, :excerpts [{:text "... meant for humans: PR descriptions and comments; Code comments; Documentation submitted to...", :matched-terms ["code"], :source :content} {:text "... you don’t understand your own PR. 1.2. Your code SHOULD have comments, but they MUST NOT be...", :matched-terms ["code"], :source :content} {:text "... by AI, become justification for bypassing guardrails and breaking invariants. So the AI can easily...", :matched-terms ["rails"], :source :content}]}]